CREATED:20260407T154642Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>Please join us for the opening demonstration of Global Conc
 epts in Motion\, a data visualization interface developed by Hugo F. Idár
 raga (PhD Candidate\, Computational Media\, Arts\, & Cultures) in convers
 ation with Christina Chia (Associate Director\, Franklin Humanities Insti
 tute). The interactive installation will be on view at the FHI from from 
 April 13 through May 15 at Bay 4\, Smith Warehouse.</p>\n\n<p>Sign up her
 e to help us with planning: <a href="https://duke.is/conceptsinmotion">ht
 tps://duke.is/conceptsinmotion</a></p>\n\n<p>Global Concepts in Motion or
 iginated from a desire to find alternative ways to re-present and activat
 e the lecture videos produced during the first year (2022) of the <a href
 ="https://fhi.duke.edu/research/entanglement-project/climate-change-decol
 onization-and-global-blackness-lab/">Climate Change\, Decolonization\, an
 d Global Blackness Lab (CCDGB)</a> at the FHI. Dense with theoretical ins
 ights and frequently poetic and speculative in orientation\, these videos
  did not fit easily in the logics of hashtags\, playlists\, and YouTube a
 lgorithms.</p>\n\n<p>We began by reflecting on how to categorize these vi
 deos in ways that could generate unexpected conceptual relationships amon
 g them. Over the past three semesters\, Hugo has developed a data visuali
 zation interface in JavaScript\, designed as a network graph through whic
 h users can explore the topics of the videos.</p>\n\n<p>In its current fo
 rm\, the interface allows for two modes of engagement: a distant reading 
 through broader categories such as race and colonialism\, and a more in-d
 epth exploration grounded in the specialized concepts of each presentatio
 n. It is intended to encourage the formation of unexpected epistemologica
 l connections\, functioning as both an analytical and political exercise 
 in data relationality.</p>
DURATION:PT1H
